---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { SITE } from "../data/site";
import { PUBLISHER_SCHEMA, SOURCE_HIERARCHY } from "../data/editorialGovernance";

const title = "Editorial Policy";
const description = "Read the Echo Buddha editorial policy covering content purpose, originality, review, corrections, and responsible limitations.";
const trustLinks = [
  {
    label: "How Echo Buddha Creates Content",
    href: "/how-echo-buddha-creates-content/",
    description: "Original writing, review boundaries, automation limits, and what the site does not claim."
  },
  {
    label: "Buddhist Sources and Citations",
    href: "/buddhist-sources-and-citations/",
    description: "How scripture, commentary, paraphrase, translation, and Echo Buddha explanation are separated."
  },
  {
    label: "Quote Attribution Policy",
    href: "/quote-attribution-policy/",
    description: "How original quote reflections, verified quotes, popular sayings, and corrections are labeled."
  },
  {
    label: "Meditation Safety",
    href: "/meditation-safety/",
    description: "Gentle practice boundaries, no treatment claims, and stop, ground, adapt, or seek-support guidance."
  },
  {
    label: "Corrections and Editorial Updates",
    href: "/corrections/",
    description: "How readers can report source, quote, typo, safety, clarity, and update concerns."
  }
];
const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "WebPage",
  name: title,
  description,
  url: `${SITE.url}/editorial-policy/`,
  publisher: PUBLISHER_SCHEMA
};
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} canonicalPath="/editorial-policy/" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="container prose">
      <span class="eyebrow">Editorial standards</span>
      <h1>Editorial Policy</h1>
      <p class="lede">How Echo Buddha creates, reviews, and corrects its educational content.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tight">
    <div class="container prose">
      <h2>Trust and Governance</h2>
      <p>
        These pages explain how Echo Buddha protects Buddhist source clarity, quote integrity, meditation safety, and
        correction pathways before broader content expansion.
      </p>
      <div class="trust-grid">
        {trustLinks.map((link) => (
          <a class="surface trust-card" href={link.href}>
            <span>{link.label}</span>
            <p>{link.description}</p>
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>

      <h2>Content Purpose</h2>
      <p>
        Echo Buddha publishes accessible Buddhist-inspired reflections, original quotes, meditation guidance, and
        practical mindfulness education. The purpose is to support thoughtful learning and daily reflection without
        presenting the website as a religious authority.
      </p>

      <h2>Publisher Accountability and Byline</h2>
      <p>
        Echo Buddha is accountable for publication and correction decisions through the public contact shown on this
        site. “Echo Buddha Editorial” is an organizational publication byline. It is not a named person, an expert
        credential, or evidence of an external review board. Echo Buddha does not publish a person's identity,
        qualifications, affiliation, or review role without verification and permission.
      </p>

      <h2>Originality</h2>
      <p>
        Articles, quote text, and reflection stories are written for Echo Buddha. The editorial process avoids copied
        passages, false quotation attributions, copyrighted imagery, and lightly rewritten material from other
        publishers.
      </p>
      <p>
        When a page refers to traditional Buddhist teachings, it uses original explanatory language and avoids long
        scripture quotations. Source notes are used to point readers toward reputable translations, teachers, and
        practice communities for deeper study.
      </p>

      <h2>Quote Origins and Attribution</h2>
      <p>
        Echo Buddha quote pages are treated as original editorial reflections unless a page clearly states another
        source. A quote must not be presented as a Buddha quote, scripture translation, historical saying, or teaching
        from a named person unless that origin has been verified against a reliable source.
        Read the <a href="/quote-attribution-policy/">Quote Attribution Policy</a> for the current labeling standard.
      </p>
      <p>
        If attribution is uncertain, the page says so plainly or remains unpublished until the uncertainty is
        resolved. Traditional Buddhist sources, translators, editions, and copyright or license notes are recorded for
        review when a page relies on them.
      </p>

      <h2>Writing and Review</h2>
      <p>
        Content is reviewed for clarity, internal consistency, respectful language, useful structure, and unsupported
        claims. Health-adjacent topics such as anxiety, sleep, stress, or emotional distress include appropriate
        limitations and do not claim that meditation cures illness or guarantees results.
      </p>
      <p>
        New and updated pages are also reviewed for search intent, duplicate-topic overlap, internal links, visible
        author information, source notes, and whether the page gives readers enough practical value to stand on its
        own.
      </p>
      <p>
        An editorial check means an internal publication check under this process. Unless a page specifically identifies a
        verified reviewer and scope, it does not mean independent monastic, academic, medical, clinical, legal, or
        institutional review.
      </p>

      <h2>Sources and Learning Context</h2>
      <p>
        Source-sensitive beginner explanations are checked against suitable primary Buddhist texts, reputable
        translations, recognized Buddhist institutions, teachers, and study communities where relevant. Pali and
        Sanskrit terms are simplified for general readers, so formal study may require more precise tradition-specific
        guidance. Read <a href="/buddhist-sources-and-citations/">Buddhist Sources and Citations</a> for more detail.
      </p>
      <ol>
        {SOURCE_HIERARCHY.map((source) => (
          <li><strong>{source.label}:</strong> {source.use}</li>
        ))}
      </ol>
      <p>
        AI-assisted and automated tools may help organize research, outline or support drafts, analyze editorial
        consistency, detect duplication, check source and link records, and run technical validation. Echo Buddha
        remains responsible for what is published. Tools do not supply authority, fabricate evidence, or replace
        human verification and qualified external guidance where it is actually needed.
        Read <a href="/how-echo-buddha-creates-content/">How Echo Buddha Creates Content</a> for the fuller process.
      </p>

      <h2>Corrections Policy</h2>
      <p>
        Substantive errors are corrected when identified. Minor wording, accessibility, linking, or formatting issues
        may also be updated to improve clarity. Article pages display stored publication dates and material-update
        dates where that metadata is available.
      </p>
      <p>
        To report an issue, email <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a> and include the page URL, the
        relevant passage, and a brief explanation. Read
        <a href="/corrections/">Corrections and Editorial Updates</a> for the review path.
      </p>

      <h2>Editorial Independence</h2>
      <p>
        Advertising placement does not determine article conclusions or quote themes. Advertising must remain
        visually distinct from editorial content and must never use language that pressures users to engage with it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Echo Buddha does not currently render ad areas. If advertising is activated later, each approved manual ad area
        must be labeled as Advertisement. Echo Buddha does not publish paid claims inside educational content or create
        fake reviews, ratings, endorsements, or spiritual authority.
      </p>
      <p>
        Echo Buddha does not currently publish affiliate links or sponsored editorial content. If a commercial
        relationship is introduced later, it must be disclosed clearly where a reader can understand its relevance;
        payment cannot purchase a source conclusion, quote attribution, or favorable review.
      </p>

      <h2>Limitations</h2>
      <p>
        Echo Buddha is an educational and reflective publication. It does not replace qualified medical, mental
        health, legal, or religious guidance. Read the <a href="/disclaimer/">Disclaimer</a> and
        <a href="/meditation-safety/">Meditation Safety</a> for more detail.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .trust-grid {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.85rem;
    margin: 1.25rem 0 2.25rem;
  }

  .trust-card {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.25rem;
    padding: 1rem;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .trust-card span {
    color: var(--color-ink);
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 1.08rem;
    font-weight: 800;
  }

  .trust-card p {
    color: var(--color-muted);
    margin: 0;
  }

  @media (min-width: 760px) {
    .trust-grid {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }
  }
</style>
